/* Logic:

1. Initialize `count = 0` to keep track of the position where the next valid element should be placed.

2. Traverse the array using a `for` loop.

3. Check whether the current element is not equal to `val`.

4. If the element is not equal to `val`, copy it to `nums[count]`.

5. Increment `count` after storing the valid element.

6. Continue until all elements are traversed.

7. Finally, return `count`, which represents the number of elements remaining after removing `val`.

Approach: Two Pointer Technique.

Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity: O(1)
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int removeElement(vector<int>& nums, int val) {

        int count = 0; // Tracks the position where the next valid element will
                       // be stored

        // Traverse through the entire array
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {

            // Check if the current element is not equal to val
            if (nums[i] != val) {

                // Move the valid element to the count position
                nums[count] = nums[i];

                // Increment count after storing the element
                count++;
            }
        }

        // Return the number of elements remaining
        return count;
    }
};
